course: arts
lesson: 17
title: Museums and galleries: how to visit and what is there
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A museum keeps important objects safe and shows them to everyone: paintings, mummies, dinosaurs, machines; a gallery mostly shows art, and some galleries also sell it.
explain: Picture a guard saying "Step back from the art, please": oils and sweat from hands slowly damage paint, and one touch is small, but millions of visitors touching would wear a painting away.
explain: Museums are huge, so the trick is to choose a few rooms and really look; many are free, so you can come back, and many have hands-on rooms, kids' trails and audio guides (recordings that explain things as you walk round).
explain: Museums also raise big questions: some objects were taken from other countries in colonial times and people argue about sending them back, and in wars museums hide their art because it holds a country's memory.
explain: People say it their way: Gen Z "slaps" means is really good, "lad" and "mate" are friendly ways to talk to a boy, and "love" is a friendly word from a nan.
fact: Museums ask people not to touch art because oils and sweat from hands damage paint over time; conservation means keeping art safe for the future.
fact: Big museums like the British Museum or the Louvre have far more than anyone can see in one day; feeling worn out from too much looking is called museum fatigue.
fact: Many objects in European museums were taken from other countries in colonial times; some countries ask for them back, people disagree about what to return, and some objects have been returned.
fact: Returning objects to the country they came from is called repatriation.
fact: Many science and children's museums have hands-on rooms made for touching, and fragile things may have copies, called replicas, that you can touch.
fact: Bright camera flashes over years can fade delicate colours and annoy visitors; many museums allow photos without flash.
fact: During wars museums hide or move their collections: in Ukraine after 2022 many museums packed and hid art, and in World War Two British paintings were hidden in a Welsh mine.
fact: Many dinosaur skeletons are casts, copies made from moulds, because real fossil bones are heavy, fragile and rare; labels usually say which parts are real.
fact: Many national museums and galleries in Britain are free to enter, paid for by taxes and donations; some charge for special exhibitions.
fact: Paintings in public museums usually belong to the museum or the country and are not for sale; at auction, art can sell for hundreds of millions.
fact: A label gives the artist, date and material, but you can enjoy art just by looking and asking yourself questions.
exercise: Harvey leans on a painting and an alarm beeps. Why must he not touch? | Oils and sweat from hands damage paint over time | The paint is still wet and will stick to his clothes | Paintings are electric and give shocks | Touching is fine, the alarms are just there for fun and noise
exercise: Aditi wants to see the whole museum in two hours. What is better? | Choose a few things and really look at them | Run through every room as fast as she can to see it all | Look only at the gift shop | Stay in one corridor all day long, looking at the floor
exercise: What is museum fatigue? | Feeling tired and bored after too much looking | A disease caught from touching very old objects | A rule that closes museums early | The special uniform that all museum guards wear
exercise: Ciaran asks why the Egyptian mummy is in London. What is true? | Many objects were taken in colonial times, and people argue about returning them | Egypt asked London to keep all its mummies for ever, and nobody has ever disagreed | Mummies can only be shown in cold countries | Nobody has ever asked for anything back from any museum in the whole world
exercise: What is repatriation? | Returning objects to the country they came from | Painting a copy of a famous work to hang instead | Cleaning an old painting carefully | Buying art at an auction for a national museum
exercise: Yuki finds a room where you can touch everything. What is it? | A hands-on room, common in science museums | A broken room the guards forgot to lock | A room where no rules apply at all | A storeroom for old rubbish
exercise: Arthur uses flash after a "no flash" sign. Why does the rule exist? | Flashes can fade delicate colours and annoy visitors | Flash makes the paintings fall off the walls | The guards just want to be bossy and spoil everybody's fun | Flash photos are illegal everywhere in the world, even at home
exercise: Nadia finds empty walls in the National Art Museum of Ukraine. Why? | The art was moved away to keep it safe in the war | The art was all sold to other countries for money | The museum simply forgot to hang it | Ukraine has no art of its own to hang on the walls
exercise: Where were British paintings hidden in World War Two? | In a mine in Wales | On a ship far out at sea | In the Tower of London | In a bank in New York
exercise: Blake asks if the dinosaur bones are real. What is common? | Many skeletons mix real bones with casts | Every dinosaur skeleton is all real bone, every piece | Every dinosaur skeleton is plastic | Dinosaurs never had bones, only shells
exercise: Saanvi's dad says galleries are for rich people. What did she find? | Many national galleries in Britain are free to enter | You need a million pounds to get in through the doors | Only people in suits can enter | Galleries are closed to children under twelve
exercise: Freya's brother asks if the painting is worth a billion. What did the guard mean by "priceless"? | It belongs to the public and is not for sale | It costs nothing at all because it is a cheap fake | It is worth exactly one billion dollars | Anyone can buy it at the gift shop on the way out
exercise: Oscar's nan says, "Never mind the label, what do you see?" What is her point? | You can enjoy art just by looking | Labels are always wrong | Reading is banned in all museums | Only experts may look at art properly
exercise: What is a replica? | A copy | A guard | A ticket | A label
exercise: What is an audio guide? | A recording that explains things as you walk | A guard who shouts the facts at visitors | A map printed on the floor | A song played at the exit as you leave
test: Your mate goes, "one little touch won't hurt it, bruv." What is the problem? | Millions of little touches would wear the art away | One touch makes it worth more | Touching is the best way to see art properly up close | Paint never wears out, however many hands touch it
test: Arthur's guard sighs, "Lad, I said no flash." What does "lad" tell you? | It's a friendly way to address a boy | The guard is angry and rude to him | It's the guard's own name | It means "you're arrested now"
test: Yuki says the museum "slaps" (is really good). What has she probably found? | A hands-on room full of buttons and games | A sign saying "do not touch anything" | An empty hall | A locked door with nobody behind it
test: What would happen if a big museum let every visitor touch the paintings for 50 years? | The paintings would be badly worn and damaged | They would look brighter and cleaner than ever | Nothing at all would change | The paintings would grow bigger
test: Two people argue: "Send the mummy home!" vs "Keep it safe here for everyone!" What is true? | People really disagree, and some objects have been returned | One side is clearly right and nobody argues | Mummies can never be moved at all, as they would crumble | Nobody cares about where objects come from
test: A cartoon shows a museum selling its Mona Lisa to the richest kid. What is wrong? | Public museum art is kept for everyone, not sold | Nothing, museums sell paintings every day | Kids are the only people who buy art | Museums sell their famous art at the gift shop on Sundays
test: Grandad says, "In a war, who cares about paintings?" What is a strong answer? | Art holds a country's memory, so people risk a lot to save it | He's right, art is only decoration | Paintings can protect people hiding behind them from bombs | Museums sell their art in wars to buy tanks and planes
test: Blake's guide says "some of each, mate." What should Blake do to know which bones are real? | Read the label | Lick the bones | Knock on them hard | Ask a dinosaur
test: Aditi's class moans "my feet are dead, miss." What would have helped? | Planning a few rooms with the map | Seeing even more rooms, but faster | Never going to museums again | Walking backwards
test: Saanvi's dad says "not for the likes of us". What is true for many national museums? | They are open to everyone, and many are free | They only let in rich people | They need a special invitation from the king | They are only for adults
test: Oscar thinks you need to know loads before you can enjoy art. What is enough to start? | Looking and asking yourself questions | Reading every book about art first | Having a degree in art | Being a museum guard for years
exam: Identify the principal reason for which visitors to a museum are requested to refrain from touching works of art. | The cumulative damage occasioned by oils and moisture from the hands | The danger of electric shock from the frames | The wetness of the paint upon the canvas, which adheres to the hands | The wish of the attendants to exercise authority
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct of museums in time of war. | Collections are frequently concealed or removed to places of safety | Collections are invariably sold to finance hostilities and the purchase of arms | Collections are left in situ without any precaution | Museums are obliged to destroy their collections
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the status of a painting held by a public museum? | It is ordinarily the property of the museum or the nation and is not offered for sale | It is offered for sale to the highest bidder upon request | It is the private property of the attendant | It may be removed by any visitor who pays the admission charge
exam: Ascertain the reason for which many skeletons of dinosaurs exhibited in museums are casts. | Genuine fossil bones are heavy, fragile and rare | Dinosaurs possessed no bones capable of preservation | Casts are more ancient than the original fossils | The display of genuine bones is prohibited by law
